WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.473 --> 00:00:02.006
- Today we're gonna be talking about

00:00:02.006 --> 00:00:04.607
what you need to know
about law school exams

00:00:04.607 --> 00:00:06.905
so you can crush your next final.

00:00:06.905 --> 00:00:09.947
So stick around and get
an edge in law school.

00:00:09.947 --> 00:00:12.530
(cheery music)

00:00:14.452 --> 00:00:16.983
Where to begin about law school exams?

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:19.491
The first thing you should
know is that almost all

00:00:19.491 --> 00:00:23.508
law school exams consist
of issue spotting essays.

00:00:23.508 --> 00:00:26.921
Sometimes called hypotheticals
or hypos for short.

00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:28.802
Sometimes you'll see multiple choice

00:00:28.802 --> 00:00:30.427
or term papers in classes,

00:00:30.427 --> 00:00:31.960
but in your first year of law school

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.932
they are almost always issue spotters.

00:00:34.932 --> 00:00:37.579
The first thing you'll notice
about an issue spotting exam

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:40.412
is that it starts with a big story.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:43.477
A big story where bad things happen.

00:00:43.477 --> 00:00:45.358
If it were a criminal law exam,

00:00:45.358 --> 00:00:48.074
your average issue
spotting exam would consist

00:00:48.074 --> 00:00:50.721
of people running around
murdering, conspiring,

00:00:50.721 --> 00:00:52.602
assaulting, and worse.

00:00:52.602 --> 00:00:54.738
If it was a property
exam, you'd probably have

00:00:54.738 --> 00:00:57.130
some sort of land dispute
between different parties

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.429
or restrictions on land.

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:02.238
If it was a contract exam,
you'd have a contract

00:01:02.238 --> 00:01:04.305
where some people would
claim it was a contract

00:01:04.305 --> 00:01:07.115
and some people would claim
it was an invalid contract.

00:01:07.115 --> 00:01:08.392
There will be lots of people

00:01:08.392 --> 00:01:10.528
doing lots of things to each other.

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:12.153
That came out wrong.

00:01:12.153 --> 00:01:15.985
At any rate, at the end of the
story there will be a prompt.

00:01:15.985 --> 00:01:18.539
The prompt usually consists
of something very vague

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:21.441
like explain the rights
of the various parties

00:01:21.441 --> 00:01:25.608
or what claims does Mrs.
Smith have against Mr. Jones?

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.545
The reason it's called
an issue spotting exam

00:01:29.545 --> 00:01:33.214
is that you're going to have
to cull through those facts

00:01:33.214 --> 00:01:36.813
to come up with the issues
that need to be discussed.

00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:40.667
Here is the main thing that
you need to understand.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.917
An issue spotter is not
about regurgitating the law

00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:47.633
or just regurgitating the facts.

00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:50.466
In a college essay exam
you get lots of points

00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:52.928
simply for writing down
things that you memorized.

00:01:52.928 --> 00:01:55.505
So if you were taking a
class on American history

00:01:55.505 --> 00:01:58.315
and the essay asked about
the American Revolution,

00:01:58.315 --> 00:02:00.358
you'd probably get lots of
points for talking about

00:02:00.358 --> 00:02:02.750
when the important battles were fought,

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.445
the causes of the revolution,

00:02:04.445 --> 00:02:06.256
the name of the British taxes,

00:02:06.256 --> 00:02:08.880
the names of the founding
fathers and etc, etc.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.152
Facts and figures don't get
you any points in law school.

00:02:13.152 --> 00:02:15.196
Generally you're analyzing the facts

00:02:15.196 --> 00:02:19.305
as a litigator would and
it's all about application.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.511
You're looking for
claims and counter claims

00:02:21.511 --> 00:02:23.485
and defenses and counter defenses

00:02:23.485 --> 00:02:25.946
that one party can bring against the other

00:02:25.946 --> 00:02:27.758
or that they can use to protect

00:02:27.758 --> 00:02:30.405
against the claims of another party.

00:02:30.405 --> 00:02:33.888
An issue spotting exam is
all about the application.

00:02:33.888 --> 00:02:37.394
You have to learn the law
and then apply the facts

00:02:37.394 --> 00:02:39.948
that are given to you in the issue spotter

00:02:39.948 --> 00:02:42.340
to the law that you know.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:46.171
In this way it's kind of like
a mechanical engineering exam.

00:02:46.171 --> 00:02:48.748
You can imagine that if
an engineering professor

00:02:48.748 --> 00:02:52.324
spent a semester
explaining how a car works,

00:02:52.324 --> 00:02:54.623
it would be fair game at the end

00:02:54.623 --> 00:02:58.919
to ask the students to design
a car for the final exam.

00:02:58.919 --> 00:03:01.380
It applies what they have learned.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.631
Law school exams are sort of the same way.

00:03:04.631 --> 00:03:09.043
You are applying the law
to a given set of facts.

00:03:09.043 --> 00:03:12.201
Memorizing the law is of course necessary,

00:03:12.201 --> 00:03:15.707
but those are the table
stakes in law school.

00:03:15.707 --> 00:03:18.191
Knowing the law will get
you through the door,

00:03:18.191 --> 00:03:21.674
but knowing the law alone
won't even get you a C,

00:03:21.674 --> 00:03:24.414
let alone a B or an A on the test.

00:03:24.414 --> 00:03:28.083
Learning how to apply the law is a skill.

00:03:28.083 --> 00:03:32.611
It takes practice, but it's
a skill that can be learned.

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:35.165
What I hate is that so
many professors claim

00:03:35.165 --> 00:03:37.534
that essay writing is an art

00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:40.273
that is incapable of being learned.

00:03:40.273 --> 00:03:43.431
In fact, although they
would not admit it to you,

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:45.660
the professor has an
interest in making you think

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.052
that it is some dark art
that can't be learned.

00:03:48.052 --> 00:03:50.699
Your professor wants you to
believe that some people have it

00:03:50.699 --> 00:03:52.532
and some people don't.

00:03:53.416 --> 00:03:55.483
But this is just nonsense.

00:03:55.483 --> 00:03:58.548
No one is born with the
skills of being able

00:03:58.548 --> 00:04:01.450
to write a law school exam, that's crazy.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:05.026
It's just a skill and just
like learning to ride a bike

00:04:05.026 --> 00:04:07.766
or making a free throw or learning guitar,

00:04:07.766 --> 00:04:10.413
you can learn the skills
that are important

00:04:10.413 --> 00:04:13.571
for a law school essay exam.

00:04:13.571 --> 00:04:16.102
Believe me, if you can
learn to pass the LSATs,

00:04:16.102 --> 00:04:19.794
then you can learn to answer
an issue spotting exam.

00:04:19.794 --> 00:04:22.859
During your first year,
they almost all tend to

00:04:22.859 --> 00:04:24.577
be issue spotting exams.

00:04:24.577 --> 00:04:27.387
But the good news is
that you can learn how

00:04:27.387 --> 00:04:29.175
to beat those tests.

00:04:29.175 --> 00:04:31.102
It's a skill, it just takes practice.

00:04:31.102 --> 00:04:33.447
Over time you'll get better at them.

00:04:33.447 --> 00:04:36.605
Don't worry if you don't
start out writing A worthy

00:04:36.605 --> 00:04:39.345
issue spotting exam essays.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.133
No one starts out that way.

00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:42.480
It just takes practice.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.546
With practice you'll get better.

00:04:44.546 --> 00:04:46.845
If you take practice
exams you can get an edge

00:04:46.845 --> 00:04:49.539
on everyone else in your class.

